1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,440
How bored are you?
你有多无聊？

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:09,150
Having nothing to do, or doing the same task again and again, can certainly be demotivating.
无所事事，或是反复做同一项任务，无疑会让人提不起劲。

3
00:00:09,150 --> 00:00:16,170
And if boredom persists, you can lose your lust for life, and it can even affect your mental health.
如果无聊感持续不散，你可能会失去对生活的热情，甚至还会影响心理健康。

4
00:00:16,170 --> 00:00:19,330
But does being bored have to be boring?
但无聊就一定得是乏味的吗？

5
00:00:19,330 --> 00:00:26,320
A lack of stimulation can lead to boredom – this is when we start to yawn and our mind wanders.
缺乏刺激会引发无聊——这时我们会开始打哈欠，思绪也会飘忽不定。

6
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:36,100
It can happen at home or at school, but it’s at work where some experts think being chronically bored can have damaging consequences.
这种情况可能发生在家里或学校，但一些专家认为，在职场中，长期的无聊会带来破坏性的后果。

7
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:42,170
This situation has been termed ‘boreout’ and is the opposite of ‘burnout’.
这种状态被称为“职业无聊症”，是“职业倦怠症”的对立面。

8
00:00:42,170 --> 00:00:53,860
Writing for BBC Worklife, Bryan Lufkin says this happens “when we are bored by our work to the point that we feel it is totally meaningless.
布莱恩·勒夫金在为BBC职场生活频道撰稿时表示，这种情况的发生是“当我们对工作感到极度无聊，以至于觉得工作完全没有意义。

9
00:00:53,860 --> 00:00:58,890
Our job seems pointless, our tasks devoid of value.”
我们的工作似乎毫无价值，手头的任务也毫无价值。”

10
00:00:58,890 --> 00:01:06,060
It can be caused by working in a demoralising physical environment or feeling under-challenged.
造成这种状态的原因可能是身处令人萎靡的工作环境，或是感觉工作缺乏挑战性。

11
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:16,400
Studies show depression from boreout can affect workers outside the office and lead to physical ailments from insomnia to headaches.
研究表明，由职业无聊症引发的抑郁情绪，会影响员工的日常生活，还会导致从失眠到头痛等一系列身体疾病。

12
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:23,560
But Bryan adds that “being able to identify it in us, is critical for tackling it.”
但布莱恩补充道：“能够意识到自己正处于这种状态，是解决问题的关键。”

13
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:29,950
When it becomes mind-numbing and extreme, some people joke about ‘dying of boredom’.
当无聊变得令人麻木且极端时，有些人会开玩笑说“无聊得要死”。

14
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:42,530
According to Luis Villazon, writing for the Science Focus website, boredom is just a state of mind which won’t kill you, though it can depress the immune system in some people.
路易斯·维拉松在为《科学焦点》网站撰稿时表示，无聊只是一种精神状态，并不会置人于死地，不过它可能会降低一些人的免疫力。

15
00:01:42,530 --> 00:01:49,550
He says “The greatest risk from boredom stems from the things you do to combat it.
他说：“无聊带来的最大风险，源于人们为了排解无聊而做出的举动。

16
00:01:49,550 --> 00:01:55,300
People who are easily bored are more likely to engage in dangerous sports [...]”, for example.
例如，容易感到无聊的人更有可能参与危险的运动……

17
00:01:55,300 --> 00:02:03,130
But some experts say boredom is a natural thing and it should be seen in a more positive light.
”但一些专家认为，无聊是一种很自然的现象，我们应该从更积极的角度看待它。

18
00:02:03,130 --> 00:02:07,840
If we see it as an opportunity, it could ignite our creativity.
如果我们把它视为一种机遇，它可能会激发我们的创造力。

19
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:16,500
And if our boredom is at work, it could force us to engage with mentors, career counsellors or even our bosses.
而如果这种无聊感源于工作，它可能会促使我们主动寻求导师、职业顾问甚至上司的帮助。

20
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:30,710
And Lotta Harju from EM Lyon Business School told the BBC, “boreout can mark a transition into something else: a different career entirely, or a different role in the organisation.
里昂商学院的洛塔·哈尔尤在接受BBC采访时表示：“职业无聊症可能标志着一种转变的开端：要么彻底转行，要么在公司内部换一个不同的岗位。

21
00:02:30,710 --> 00:02:33,720
If people only take its cue.”
前提是如果人们愿意听从它发出的信号。”
